---
import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { gameDefinitions } from "../config/games";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Game Save Locations & Compatibility | Save Scum"
  heading="Find the folder. Protect the progress."
  description="Understand where games store saves, how Save Scum discovers local folders, and the difference between a location template and verified game compatibility."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Games & save folders", href: "/games/" }]}
  eyebrow="YOUR LIBRARY. YOUR FILES."
>
  <p>
    Save Scum can monitor ordinary local save directories without Steam. It does
    not parse every game’s save format or capture server-only progress. A
    bundled discovery definition helps find a folder; it does not certify every
    edition, operating system, or mod setup.
  </p>
  <h2>Start with the game’s own documentation</h2>
  <p>
    Save paths vary by operating system, store, profile, and game version. Check
    publisher instructions first. Make a normal in-game save, exit, and verify
    that the expected files changed. Some games spread profile data and world
    files across more than one location; understand the complete set before
    restoring.
  </p>
  <p>
    Common starting points include <code>Documents</code>,{" "}
    <code>Saved Games</code>, Windows <code>AppData</code>, macOS{" "}
    <code>Library/Application Support</code>, and Linux configuration
    directories. Steam userdata and Proton prefixes may also contain saves. None
    is a universal folder for every PC game.
  </p>
  <h2>Bundled discovery templates</h2>
  <p>
    The current app includes {gameDefinitions.length} named definitions. The
    paths below are hints copied from the application’s configuration, not
    tested compatibility claims. A wildcard <code>*</code> stands for a profile
    or account-specific directory. Verify the files before using a match.
  </p>
  {gameDefinitions.map((game) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{game.name}</h3>
      <dl class="path-list">
        {Object.entries(game.paths)
          .filter(([, paths]) => paths.length)
          .map(([platform, paths]) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                {platform === "macos"
                  ? "macOS"
                  : platform === "windows"
                    ? "Windows"
                    : "Linux / Proton"}{" "}
                location template
              </dt>
              <dd>
                {paths.map((path) => (
                  <div>
                    <code>{path}</code>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))}
      </dl>
    </section>
  ))}
  <h2>Installed does not mean located</h2>
  <p>
    The app can discover installed Steam games from common library locations. If
    the save path is unknown, it labels the game as installed and asks you to
    choose the actual save folder. An installation directory is never
    automatically assumed to hold your progress. Custom Steam libraries and
    unrecognized locations can be added manually.
  </p>
  <h2>Before you trust a backup</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Confirm the watched directory contains the current profile’s real files.
    </li>
    <li>Capture a snapshot and inspect its file count and path.</li>
    <li>
      Keep an independent copy while testing a restore with the game closed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Verify that the game loads the expected state and that cloud sync does not
      replace it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    See <a href="/how-it-works/">the setup walkthrough</a> or{" "}
    <a href="/guides/how-to-back-up-steam-saves/">the Steam backup guide</a> for
    more detail. Product and game names identify compatibility contexts only;
    Save Scum is not affiliated with Valve or the listed publishers.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
